"""12 条作物健康档案的英文（译自校准版中文，勿增删信息）。"""
EN = {
    "健康番茄": {
        "morphology_en": "Leaves are spreading pinnately compound, deep green and glossy, with intact margins free of spots, yellowing, crinkling or deformity. Stems are thick and erect with uniform internode length (10–15 cm) and no excessive vegetative growth. The root system is well developed, with many white, tender fibrous roots showing no browning. Inflorescences flower normally, the corolla is bright yellow and pollination is good. Fruit are uniform in size, well shaped and bright in colour (red/pink/yellow), free of lesions, cracking and damage. The whole plant grows evenly, without yellowing, wilting or stunting.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "1) Temperature: a warm-season crop; the optimum temperature for assimilation is 20–30 °C; during the growing period 25–28 °C by day and 15–18 °C at night; during flowering and fruiting 25–30 °C by day and 15–18 °C at night; below 15 °C or above 35 °C pollination and fertilisation are poor; the optimum root-zone temperature is 20–22 °C, and root growth is inhibited below 13 °C. 2) Light: light-loving; the light saturation point is about 70000 lx, and 12–14 h of light per day favours flowering and fruiting. 3) Humidity: air relative humidity of 50–65% is suitable (in protected cultivation 60–70%, controlled by drip irrigation under the film plus ventilation); soil water-holding capacity 60–80%. 4) Soil: deep, loose and fertile loam or sandy loam with good drainage and irrigation, pH 6.0–7.0 being suitable, with organic matter content above 1.5%. 5) Nutrition: balanced N:P:K, with additional potassium applied during the fruiting period.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Select excellent disease-resistant and stress-tolerant cultivars and raise disease-free vigorous seedlings; 2) seed disinfection (soaking in 10% trisodium phosphate for 20–30 min to prevent viral diseases and hot-water treatment at 55 °C to prevent fungal and bacterial diseases); 3) carry out crop rotation with non-solanaceous crops for more than 3 years; 4) raised-bed cultivation (bed height 15–20 cm), plastic mulching and drip irrigation under the film; 5) rational close planting (3000–3500 plants per mu (1 mu ≈ 1/15 ha) for large-fruited types, cherry tomatoes slightly denser), with timely pruning and sucker removal and vine tying; 6) apply sufficient decomposed organic fertiliser (3000–5000 kg per mu) as base fertiliser; after transplanting and seedling establishment apply a seedling-promoting fertiliser, and apply a heavy fruit-promoting fertiliser after the first truss has swollen; during the fruiting period apply 5–10 kg per mu of high-potassium water-soluble fertiliser every 10–15 days; 7) avoid over-application of nitrogen fertiliser.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) Soil disinfection before transplanting (broadcasting calcium cyanamide (lime nitrogen) or dazomet); 2) apply medicated soil in the planting holes at transplanting to prevent root diseases; 3) during the growing period, according to the occurrence of pests and diseases, spray protective fungicides (mancozeb, chlorothalonil) in a targeted way to prevent early blight and leaf mould, and use difenoconazole, azoxystrobin, etc. at the early stage of disease; 4) when using insecticides against aphids, tobacco whitefly and cotton bollworm, pay attention to rotating chemicals; 5) strictly observe the pesticide pre-harvest interval and stop applying pesticides 7–14 days before harvest.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Regulate temperature and humidity in protected cultivation by ventilation and wet pads, maintaining 25–28 °C by day and 15–18 °C at night, with air humidity controlled at about 60%; 2) protect and release natural enemies: Encarsia formosa against tobacco whitefly, predatory mites against spider mites and Trichogramma against cotton bollworm; 3) apply bio-pesticides: Bt against lepidopteran pests and Bacillus subtilis against fungal diseases; 4) intercrop companion crops (such as alliums) to repel pests; 5) maintain field biodiversity and reduce the use of broad-spectrum insecticides.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Raise seedlings in insect-proof netting houses (40–60 mesh) to exclude tobacco whitefly and aphids; 2) hang yellow sticky traps in the greenhouse (20–30 traps per mu) to trap and kill aphids and whiteflies; 3) lay silver-grey reflective mulch to repel aphids; 4) disinfect the facility by high-temperature closed-house treatment (soil solarisation) during the fallow period (sealed at 35–38 °C for 3–5 days); 5) promptly remove old leaves, diseased leaves and spent flowers by hand to improve ventilation and light penetration.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) During the seedling establishment period after transplanting (7–10 days), check the survival rate and root growth; 2) during flowering and fruiting, inspect leaf colour, stem diameter and fruit set daily; 3) automatically monitor temperature and humidity in the protected facility: ventilate when the daytime temperature exceeds 30 °C, keep warm when it falls below 15 °C, and ventilate to remove moisture when humidity exceeds 70%; 4) regularly survey for centres of pest and disease occurrence; 5) keep field production records of fertilisation, pesticide application and harvest dates; 6) test soil pH once a year and maintain it at 6.0–7.0.",
    },
    "健康草莓": {
        "morphology_en": "Plants are compact, sturdy and uniform, with thick new crowns; trifoliate leaves are spreading with elliptical leaflets, bright green and glossy, with intact serrated margins, free of purple-brown spots, powdery mildew and grey mould; petioles are erect and robust, without lesions or deformity; inflorescences are horizontal to slightly erect, with many flowers, good pollination and no deformed flowers; fruit are uniform, bright red and glossy with firm flesh, free of soft rot and lesions; the root system is well developed with many white, tender fibrous roots, free of red stele root rot.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "Prefers a cool and mild climate; optimum growing temperature 20–25 °C by day and 10–15 °C at night; optimum temperature for photosynthesis about 20 °C, with a relatively high photosynthetic rate between 15 and 27 °C; suitable soil temperature for roots 18–23 °C, with root growth inhibited above 25 °C or below 13 °C; flower-bud differentiation requires low temperature and short days, with an optimum of 10–17 °C (ceasing below 5 °C) and dormancy below 5 °C. Loose, fertile sandy loam with convenient drainage and irrigation is suitable, at slightly acidic pH 5.5–6.5, with organic matter content of 1.5%–2% or more; air relative humidity 60%–70%. It is light-loving but relatively tolerant of lateral shading.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Use certified disease-free vigorous seedlings and locally adapted cultivars (such as Benihoppe, Akihime and Sweet Charlie); 2) avoid continuous cropping with solanaceous and cucurbitaceous crops and practise 2–3 years of crop rotation; 3) use raised beds (30–40 cm) with plastic mulching; 4) apply 2500–3000 kg of decomposed organic fertiliser per mu as base fertiliser, together with a compound NPK fertiliser; 5) top-dress by integrated water and fertiliser management at about N:P:K = (1–1.2):0.5:(1.4–2), starting with small, frequent applications after flower-bud emergence; 6) plant at a rational density (8000–10000 plants per mu) and promptly remove old leaves, diseased leaves and runners; 7) use bee pollination (1 hive of bees per mu) to improve fruit set.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) Carry out soil disinfection before transplanting (dazomet, chloropicrin or solarisation) to control Fusarium wilt and root rot; 2) during the growing period, spray preventively with mancozeb, difenoconazole, etc. to control grey mould, powdery mildew and leaf spot; 3) spray imidacloprid or thiamethoxam to control aphids and abamectin/spirodiclofen to control spider mites; 4) strictly observe the pesticide pre-harvest interval during the flowering and fruiting period, giving priority to bio-pesticides.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Release predatory mites (such as Neoseiulus cucumeris) to control spider mites and Encarsia formosa to control whitefly; 2) apply bio-pesticides such as Bacillus subtilis, Trichoderma and berberine; 3) regulate temperature and humidity by ventilation in protected cultivation, with a daytime temperature of 20–24 °C and a night temperature of 10–12 °C; 4) maintain moderate cover cropping between rows to protect natural enemies; 5) use drip irrigation instead of flood irrigation to reduce leaf wetness.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Raise seedlings in insect-proof netting houses to exclude aphids and whiteflies; 2) use yellow sticky traps to trap and kill aphids and whiteflies and silver-grey reflective mulch to repel aphids; 3) use plastic mulching (black or bicolour) to suppress weeds and reduce humidity; 4) disinfect the facility by high-temperature closed-house treatment (soil solarisation); 5) carry out manual flower and fruit thinning and remove old and diseased leaves.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) Inspect plant growth daily, observing leaf colour, leaf shape and the state of flowers and fruit; 2) focus monitoring on grey mould (high humidity during the flowering and fruiting period), powdery mildew, anthracnose, spider mites and aphids; 3) monitor temperature and humidity in the greenhouse, keeping the daytime temperature no higher than 28 °C and the night temperature no lower than 5 °C; 4) regularly test seedlings for virus infection; 5) keep production records documenting all cultivation operations and pesticide applications.",
    },
    "健康大豆": {
        "morphology_en": "Trifoliate leaves are spreading and deep green, with intact leaflets free of lesions; stems are thick and erect, resistant to lodging; the root system is well developed, bearing numerous pink nodules; pod set is abundant, pods are plump and free of pod borer damage; seeds are plump with glossy seed coats.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "Soybean is a warm-season, short-day crop; the optimum temperature for growth is 20–30 °C and for the flowering and podding stage 25–28 °C; water demand is relatively high during flowering, podding and seed filling. Neutral to slightly alkaline loam with pH 6.5–7.5 is suitable; acid soils need lime application for adjustment. Rhizobia favour a neutral to slightly alkaline environment, and excessive acidity inhibits nitrogen fixation.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Select high-yielding, disease-resistant cultivars; 2) sow at the appropriate time and plant at a rational density; 3) soil testing and formulated fertilisation: producing 100 kg of soybean grain requires the uptake of about 6.5–9.5 kg of pure nitrogen, 1.5–4.2 kg of phosphorus pentoxide (P2O5) and 3.2–6.2 kg of potassium oxide (K2O), i.e. N:P2O5:K2O of about 4:1:2 (2.9:1:1.8 in some references); because rhizobial nitrogen fixation can supply 50–60% of the soybean nitrogen requirement, apply little nitrogen at the seedling stage, emphasise phosphorus and potassium, and top-dress a small amount of nitrogen at early flowering depending on growth; 4) treat seed with rhizobium inoculant before sowing to promote nodule formation; 5) thin and finalise the stand in time, carry out inter-row cultivation and weeding, and prevent lodging and premature senescence later.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) Treat seed with chemicals before sowing (fludioxonil, thiamethoxam, etc.) to control root rot, soil-inhabiting insects and seedling-stage aphids; 2) spray during the growing period to control aphids, limabean pod borer, pod-feeding caterpillars and spider mites; 3) spray fungicides at the early stage of disease in the later period to control downy mildew, purple seed stain and leaf spot; 4) strictly observe the pesticide pre-harvest interval.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Protect and use Trichogramma to control the limabean pod borer and ladybirds and lacewings to control aphids; 2) apply bio-pesticides such as Bt and Beauveria bassiana; 3) rotate with gramineous crops to reduce pests, diseases and continuous-cropping problems; 4) manage weeds along field edges to reduce alternate hosts of pests and diseases.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Practise rational crop rotation (soybean → corn → wheat); 2) deep-plough the soil in autumn to bury diseased residues and overwintering pupae; 3) use black-light lamps or sex pheromone lures to trap and kill adults of the limabean pod borer and pod-feeding caterpillars; 4) weed by hand.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) After emergence, check the crop and replant gaps in time; 2) survey aphids and downy mildew at flowering; 3) during podding, monitor the egg numbers of the limabean pod borer and pod-feeding caterpillars and the rate of pod damage; 4) prevent drought and premature senescence during seed filling; 5) measure and estimate yield before harvest.",
    },
    "健康树莓": {
        "morphology_en": "The plant is an open bush consisting of biennial fruiting canes (floricanes) and current-year vegetative canes (primocanes); canes are erect or semi-erect, brown, with fine and evenly distributed prickles; leaves are odd-pinnately compound with 3–5 leaflets, dark green, glossy above and greyish-white beneath; flower buds are plump and the flowers white; fruit are plump, bright red (or yellow/black) aggregate drupelets with a glossy surface that separate easily from the receptacle (red raspberry); the root system is well developed and shallow, with strong suckering ability, and there is no cane-base canker or root rot.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "Prefers cool conditions and light and is relatively cold-hardy; optimum growing temperature 15–25 °C (10–25 °C acceptable); during dormancy it tolerates low temperatures of -10 °C to -20 °C; the chill requirement is about 800–1700 chill hours (below 7.2 °C) to complete dormancy. Loose, fertile sandy loam or gravelly loam with good drainage is best, at slightly acidic pH 5.5–6.5 (iron-deficiency yellowing readily occurs above pH 7.0 and growth is poor above 7.5); soil organic matter above 3%–4%. It does not tolerate waterlogging; roots are shallow, drought tolerance is moderate, and adequate water is needed during fruit development.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Select locally adapted cultivars and include pollinator cultivars; 2) in the autumn of the year before planting, deep-plough and improve the soil, work in decomposed organic fertiliser and adjust pH according to soil testing; 3) use a spacing of 0.5–0.8 m × 2.0–2.5 m and set up a trellis to tie the canes; 4) autumn pruning: thin out weak, thin, diseased and dead biennial canes, retaining 8–12 vigorous fruiting canes per square metre; 5) top-dress in split applications before spring budbreak and during fruit swelling, using nitrogen to promote spring canes and phosphorus and potassium to fill out the fruit; 6) drain promptly in the rainy season and use drip irrigation in dry periods; 7) harvest in several pickings as the fruit ripens.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) Spray lime sulphur at 3–5 degrees Baumé during dormancy for orchard sanitation to eliminate overwintering pathogens; 2) during the growing period, spray preventively with mancozeb, difenoconazole, etc. to control cane blight, leaf spot and anthracnose; 3) spray imidacloprid or thiamethoxam to control aphids (virus vectors) and cyhalothrin, etc. to control raspberry beetle; 4) strictly observe the pre-harvest interval during the fruit harvest period and stop applying pesticides 7–14 days before harvest.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Protect and use natural enemies such as ladybirds, lacewings, parasitic wasps and insectivorous birds; 2) apply biological agents such as Bacillus subtilis and Beauveria bassiana; 3) practise cover cropping between rows (white clover, etc.) to improve the rhizosphere ecology; 4) apply more microbial fertiliser and decomposed organic fertiliser and avoid continuous cropping; 5) avoid broad-spectrum insecticides that harm natural enemies.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Carry out winter orchard sanitation, pruning out diseased and dead canes and burning them in a centralised way; 2) set up trellises and tie the canes to improve ventilation and light penetration; 3) use bird netting or bagging during fruit ripening to prevent bird damage; 4) carry out manual cane thinning and flower and fruit thinning; 5) use plastic film or straw mulching to conserve soil moisture and suppress weeds.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) Observe the uniformity of budbreak in spring; 2) during the growing period, regularly inspect the cane bases (cane blight) and leaves (leaf spot, aphids, raspberry beetle); 3) monitor fruit flies and bird damage during fruit ripening; 4) in autumn and winter, survey plant vigour and cane numbers to guide pruning and fertilisation; 5) regularly test soil pH and organic matter.",
    },
    "健康马铃薯": {
        "morphology_en": "Pinnately compound leaves are deep green, flat and spreading; stems are thick and erect with a uniform plant habit; inflorescences open normally; tubers have smooth skin, uniform shape and shallow eyes, with normal flesh colour (yellow/white), free of lesions, insect damage and internal brown necrosis; the root system is well developed.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "Potato prefers cool conditions and does not tolerate high temperatures: the optimum temperature for stem and leaf growth is 17–21 °C and the optimum soil temperature for tuber swelling 16–18 °C; above 25 °C tuber growth stops and degeneration readily occurs; short days and a large diurnal temperature difference favour tuber swelling. Deep, loose, well-aerated sandy loam with good drainage and irrigation is required, at a suitable pH of 5.5–6.5 (slightly acidic). The tuber initiation stage (full flowering to the swelling stage) is the critical period for water demand, and field capacity should be maintained at 70–80%.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Certified disease-free seed tubers (class I or foundation seed) must be used; this is the prerequisite for disease prevention and high yield; 2) rotate for more than 3 years with non-solanaceous crops (gramineous and leguminous crops); 3) deep and thorough tillage with raised-bed cultivation, bed height 15–20 cm; 4) apply sufficient base fertiliser, with more organic fertiliser and phosphorus and potassium, avoiding over-application of nitrogen; 5) plant at a rational density and earth up in several rounds during the growing period to prevent tubers from being exposed to light and turning green; 6) warm the seed and pre-sprout before sowing to produce short, sturdy sprouts.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) After cutting the seed tubers, treat them with fungicides such as thiophanate-methyl and fludioxonil and sow after wound periderm has formed at the cut surfaces; 2) start protective spraying at the bud stage to protect against late blight and early blight; 3) spray to control aphids (virus vectors) and potato tuber moth; 4) cut the haulm 7–10 days before harvest to reduce infection of the tubers by late blight.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Apply microbial fertiliser to improve the soil microflora; 2) rotate with gramineous and leguminous crops to reduce soil-borne diseases; 3) protect the natural enemies of aphids (ladybirds, lacewings); 4) apply bio-pesticides such as Bacillus subtilis to prevent late blight.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Strictly select certified disease-free seed tubers and discard diseased and rotten tubers; 2) pre-sprout and sort before sowing, discarding weak, spindly sprouts; 3) earth up properly to prevent tubers from turning green through exposure; 4) after harvest, cure the tubers in a ventilated, cool, shaded place for wound healing, and strictly discard diseased and damaged tubers before cellaring; 5) maintain 1–4 °C with ventilation during storage.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) After emergence, check the crop and fill gaps; 2) from the bud stage, systematically monitor for primary infection foci of late blight; 3) ensure a uniform water supply during tuber swelling to prevent tuber cracking; 4) measure and estimate yield before harvest; 5) regularly check for rotting tubers before cellaring and during storage.",
    },
    "健康甜椒": {
        "morphology_en": "Leaves are long-ovate to ovate, deep green and glossy, with intact surfaces free of spots, yellowing and curling. Stems are thick with a good branching habit (normal development of the first-fork fruit, the second-fork pair and the four-fruit tier). The root system is well developed but relatively shallow, with many white, tender fibrous roots, and there are no dead or wilted plants. Fruit are bell-shaped or square bell-shaped with thick flesh and a smooth, bright surface (dark green when mature-green and bright red when ripe), free of lesions, blossom-end rot and sunscald. The plants grow robustly overall, without excessive vegetative growth or stunting.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "1) Temperature: warm-season and not cold-tolerant; the optimum growing temperature is 22–28 °C by day and 15–18 °C at night; during flowering and fruiting 22–28 °C by day; above 32 °C pollen viability declines and pollination and fertilisation are impeded, causing flower and fruit drop; below 15 °C flowering and fruiting are poor; the optimum germination temperature is 28–30 °C. 2) Light: the light requirement is moderate and it tolerates weak light; 10–12 h of light per day favours flowering and fruiting; excessively strong light readily causes sunscald. 3) Humidity: it prefers relatively dry air, with air relative humidity of 50–60% being suitable (excessive humidity readily causes disease, while excessive dryness affects flowering and fruiting); relative soil water-holding capacity 55–75%; it tolerates neither drought nor waterlogging. 4) Soil: deep, loose and fertile loam or sandy loam with good drainage and irrigation, pH 6.0–7.0 being suitable, with high organic matter content. 5) Nutrition: the fertiliser requirement is lower than that of tomato; waterlogging must be avoided, and extra potassium is applied after fruit set.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Select disease-resistant cultivars (resistant to Phytophthora blight, bacterial wilt and viral diseases); 2) seed disinfection: hot-water treatment at 55 °C for 30 min, or soaking in 10% trisodium phosphate for 20–30 min to prevent viral diseases; 3) raised-bed cultivation (bed height 15–20 cm), plastic mulching and drip irrigation under the film; 4) apply sufficient decomposed organic fertiliser (3000–4000 kg per mu) as base fertiliser; 5) after transplanting and seedling establishment, restrict water to harden the seedlings and encourage deep rooting; once the first-fork fruit has set (fruit the size of a chopstick tip), begin a uniform water supply to keep the soil moist, avoiding alternating drought and flooding (to prevent blossom-end rot and fruit cracking); 6) plant at a rational density (3000–4000 plants per mu) and promptly remove the side shoots below the first-fork fruit and old leaves; 7) drain promptly in the rainy season to prevent root waterlogging and dead plants.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) Soil disinfection before transplanting (to prevent bacterial wilt, Phytophthora blight and root-knot nematode); 2) during the growing period, according to disease occurrence, spray dimethomorph or metalaxyl-mancozeb at the early stage of disease to prevent Phytophthora blight, and copper hydroxide or zinc thiazole to prevent bacterial spot; 3) use insecticides to control aphids, tobacco whitefly, tobacco budworm and cotton bollworm; 4) strictly observe the pesticide pre-harvest interval during the flowering and fruiting period, stopping application 10–15 days before harvest; 5) use copper formulations with caution at the seedling stage to avoid phytotoxicity.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Regulate temperature and humidity by ventilation in protected cultivation, maintaining 22–28 °C by day and 15–18 °C at night, with air humidity controlled at 50–60%; 2) protect and release natural enemies: Encarsia formosa against tobacco whitefly, predatory mites against spider mites and Trichogramma against tobacco budworm; 3) apply bio-pesticides: Bt against lepidopteran pests and Bacillus subtilis and kasugamycin against fungal and bacterial diseases; 4) use drip irrigation rather than flood irrigation to reduce disease spread; 5) avoid continuous cropping with solanaceous crops.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Cover the seedling greenhouse with 40–60 mesh insect-proof netting to exclude tobacco whitefly and aphids; 2) hang yellow sticky traps in the greenhouse (20–30 traps per mu) to trap and kill aphids and whiteflies; 3) lay silver-grey reflective mulch to repel aphids; 4) disinfect the facility by high-temperature closed-house treatment (soil solarisation) during the fallow period; 5) carry out manual pruning and sucker removal and remove old and diseased leaves, operating after the dew has dried; 6) provide appropriate shading during periods of strong light to prevent sunscald.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) During the seedling establishment period after transplanting (7–10 days), check the survival rate and any dead plants; 2) during flowering and fruit set, focus on monitoring temperature (ventilate and cool when above 32 °C to prevent flower drop) and humidity (remove moisture when above 60%); 3) from the first-fork fruit stage to the fruiting period, regularly survey the occurrence of Phytophthora blight, bacterial spot and tobacco budworm; 4) when wilted plants are found, immediately check the roots (bacterial wilt, root rot); 5) test soil pH every year and maintain it at 6.0–7.0; 6) keep production records of fertilisation, pesticide application and harvest.",
    },
    "健康桃子": {
        "morphology_en": "Leaves are lanceolate, 12–15 cm long, with acuminate tips and finely serrate margins; they are deep green and glossy, with beard-like hairs in the vein axils beneath, and free of yellowing, mottling, shot holes and lesions. Current-year shoots are reddish-brown on the sunny side and green on the shaded side, with uniform internodes (2–3 cm long), plump ovate flower buds and a moderate flower-bud to leaf-bud ratio. Fruit are uniform in size (150–350 g depending on cultivar), well shaped, with smooth skin and intact fuzz, colouring in accordance with cultivar characteristics (pink/red/yellow-white), and firm flesh with a rich, sweet flavour. The root system is well developed with many white, tender fibrous roots, free of decay and crown gall. Tree vigour is moderate to strong, with no gummosis, canker or premature defoliation.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "1) Temperature: mean annual temperature 12–17 °C and mean monthly temperature in summer around 25 °C; optimum temperature during the growing season 18–25 °C; during flowering the mean temperature must remain stable above 10 °C, with 12–14 °C being optimal for normal pollination and fertilisation; in winter 750–1200 chill hours (below 7.2 °C), depending on cultivar, are required to complete the chill requirement, otherwise budbreak and flowering are uneven; the extreme winter minimum should not fall below -22 to -25 °C. 2) Light: extremely light-demanding, requiring annual sunshine hours of more than 1200–1800 h; insufficient light leads to excessive shoot growth, few flower buds, low fruit set and poor colouring. 3) Soil: deep (≥60 cm), loose and fertile sandy loam or gravelly loam, pH 6.0–7.0 (slightly acidic to neutral), total salt content <0.1%, and a groundwater table below 1 m. 4) Water: drought-tolerant but susceptible to waterlogging; soil water-holding capacity of 60–70% is suitable during the growing period; water demand is higher during fruit swelling, and moderate water restriction at maturity improves quality. 5) Nutrition: apply sufficient decomposed organic fertiliser in autumn (2000–3000 kg per mu), together with a compound fertiliser at N:P:K = 1:0.5:1, avoiding over-application of nitrogen.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Select excellent cultivars suited to the local climate and provide pollinator trees (main cultivar:pollinator = 2:1 or 4:1); 2) apply base fertiliser in autumn (months 9–10, September to October, mainly organic fertiliser), with split top-dressing before flowering, after flowering and during fruit swelling; 3) carry out rational training and pruning (open-centre or Y shape) and thin flowers and fruit to control the crop load (retain 1–2 fruit on long fruiting shoots and 1 fruit on medium fruiting shoots); 4) use ridge cultivation (ridge height 20–30 cm) to prevent waterlogging; 5) bag the fruit to improve external quality; 6) harvest at the proper time and avoid over-ripening.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) During dormancy (before budbreak), spray lime sulphur at 3–5 degrees Baumé to eliminate overwintering sources of pests and diseases; 2) during the growing period, spray protective fungicides (mancozeb) according to forecasts to prevent brown rot, anthracnose and shot hole, and systemic curative fungicides (difenoconazole, thiophanate-methyl) to control leaf diseases; 3) use insecticides to control green peach aphid (imidacloprid/thiamethoxam), peach fruit moth (chlorantraniliprole) and spider mites (abamectin/spirodiclofen); 4) strictly observe the pre-harvest interval and stop applying pesticides 2–3 weeks before harvest.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Practise cover cropping between orchard rows (white clover, hairy vetch), mowing and mulching the tree rows; 2) protect and use natural enemies such as ladybirds, lacewings, hoverflies and parasitic wasps to control aphids and fruit borers; 3) apply bio-pesticides (Bt, matrine, azadirachtin, Bacillus subtilis); 4) apply more microbial fertiliser (EM and Trichoderma) to improve the rhizosphere micro-ecology; 5) avoid highly toxic, high-residue pesticides.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Carry out winter orchard sanitation, scraping off old and loose bark and removing fallen leaves and fruit for centralised burning; 2) whitewash the trunks (quicklime:sulphur:water = 10:1:40) to prevent freezing injury and pests; 3) bag the fruit; 4) use sugar-vinegar liquid or sex pheromone lures to trap and kill adult fruit borers; 5) lay reflective film to promote fruit colouring; 6) plant windbreaks in orchards exposed to wind.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) At budbreak, survey the rate of flower-bud swelling and the effectiveness of frost protection; 2) at flowering, observe pollination and fertilisation and late-frost damage; 3) during fruit swelling, regularly survey pests and diseases (shot hole, brown rot, fruit borers); 4) before harvest, measure yield and sample fruit quality; 5) in autumn and winter, assess tree vigour (shoot length, weight of 100 leaves) to guide pruning and fertilisation.",
    },
    "健康葡萄": {
        "morphology_en": "Leaves are palmate with 3–5 lobes, deep green and glossy, flat, free of lesions and yellowing; new shoots are robust with uniform internodes and plump winter buds; clusters are uniform, with berries of even size and consistent colouring and an intact bloom, free of lesions and cracking; the root system is well developed with many fibrous roots, vine vigour is moderate to strong, and the fruiting canes are well filled.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "Grape is a warm-temperate plant; budbreak begins when the temperature stabilises above 10 °C; the optimum temperature for shoot growth and fruit ripening is 20–30 °C, and 20–25 °C with a large diurnal temperature difference is best during fruit ripening; abundant light is required. Loose, fertile sandy loam with good drainage is suitable, at pH 6.0–7.0 (optimum around 6.5; below pH 5.5 aluminium and manganese toxicity readily occurs, and above pH 7.5 the availability of iron, manganese and zinc declines). Water: demand is relatively high at budbreak and during berry swelling (field capacity about 70%), while water should be restricted at flowering and during fruit ripening (field capacity 50%–60%); sprinkler irrigation is contraindicated at flowering.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Select excellent table or wine grape cultivars adapted to the area and provide pollinator cultivars rationally; 2) apply sufficient decomposed organic fertiliser before planting; 3) erect trellises and train the vines (trellis, pergola or V-shaped trellis) with rational winter pruning and summer pinching and shoot thinning; 4) thin flowers and fruit to control the crop load; 5) as a reference, apply 6–8 kg of pure nitrogen per tonne of yield, together with phosphorus and potassium and calcium, magnesium and boron; 6) drain promptly in the rainy season to remove waterlogging.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) During dormancy (before bud swelling), spray lime sulphur at 3–5 degrees Baumé to eliminate overwintering pests and diseases; 2) during the growing period, use protective fungicides such as Bordeaux mixture and mancozeb to prevent downy mildew, powdery mildew, black pox and black rot; 3) at the early stage of disease, choose targeted chemicals such as dimethomorph (downy mildew) and difenoconazole/tebuconazole (powdery mildew, black pox, white rot); 4) spray to control clearwing moths, thrips, spider mites, etc.; 5) strictly observe the pre-harvest interval.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Sow cover crops between rows (white clover, etc.) to improve the orchard microclimate and reduce sunscald and rain splash; 2) protect natural enemies such as ladybirds, lacewings and parasitic wasps; 3) apply bio-pesticides such as Bacillus subtilis; 4) apply more bio-organic fertiliser to improve the rhizosphere micro-ecology.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Carry out winter orchard sanitation, removing diseased canes and fallen leaves; 2) bag the fruit; 3) whitewash the trunks to prevent freezing injury and sunscald; 4) promote rain-shelter cultivation in the rainy southern regions; 5) carry out manual pinching, cluster thinning and berry thinning.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) At budbreak, check new shoot emergence and primary infection of downy mildew; 2) at flowering, check inflorescence development and grey mould; 3) during berry swelling, regularly survey downy mildew, powdery mildew, black rot and anthracnose; 4) at ripening, assess colouring, sugar content and diseases; 5) in autumn and winter, survey vine vigour to guide winter pruning and fertilisation.",
    },
    "健康玉米": {
        "morphology_en": "Leaves are flat and broad, deep green and glossy, with a prominent midrib and intact margins free of lesions. Stalks are thick with well-developed mechanical tissue, standing erect without lodging; prop roots (aerial roots) are well developed and numerous. Tassels are well branched with abundant pollen and uniform pollen shedding; ear silks are exserted evenly and uniformly. Ears are uniform in size and position, with plump, regularly arranged kernels and no bare tips or missing kernels. The root system is well developed with white, tender roots, and the whole plant grows uniformly, free of yellowing, wilting, lesions and insect feeding tunnels.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "A warm- and light-loving crop; the optimum growing temperature is 22–30 °C (25–28 °C is best from jointing to grain filling), and the whole growth period requires an accumulated active temperature (above 10 °C) of 2000–3000 °C. Fertile, loose loam to sandy loam with good drainage and irrigation is suitable, at pH 6.0–7.0. Water demand is high: the period from the large trumpet stage to grain filling (with tasselling as the critical water period) is the peak water demand, accounting for more than 50% of total water consumption. Planting density is determined by soil fertility and cultivar characteristics: 4000–5500 plants per mu for density-tolerant compact cultivars, 3000–4000 plants per mu for flat-leaf types or moderately fertile land, and about 4500–5000 plants per mu for conventional high-yield fields.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Select high-yielding, disease-resistant hybrid cultivars; 2) sow at the appropriate time (when the soil temperature at 5–10 cm has stably exceeded 10–12 °C), with precision sowing for full emergence in one pass; 3) plant at a rational density, with the final stand determined by cultivar characteristics and soil fertility; 4) soil testing and formulated fertilisation: at the yield level per mu the total applications are nitrogen (N) 16–18 kg per mu, phosphorus (P2O5) 7–9 kg per mu and potassium (K2O) 9–11 kg per mu, of which 60%–70% of the nitrogen is top-dressed in 3–4 split applications at the jointing, large trumpet, silking and grain-filling stages, with a heavy ear fertiliser at the large trumpet stage; 5) thin and finalise the stand in time, removing weak plants and keeping vigorous ones; 6) carry out inter-row cultivation and weeding, and earth up to store water and prevent lodging; 7) harvest late at the proper time (when the husk leaves are dry and loose and the kernel milk line has disappeared) to fully exploit the grain-filling potential.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) Before sowing, use seed coating or chemical seed treatment to control head smut, stalk rot and soil-inhabiting insects; 2) at the large trumpet stage, broadcast granules into the leaf whorl (such as phoxim or chlorantraniliprole granules) to control the Asian corn borer; 3) in the middle and late periods, as part of the combined 'one spray, multiple promotion' treatment, spray fungicides such as difenoconazole, azoxystrobin and pyraclostrobin to prevent northern and southern leaf blights, grey leaf spot and rust, and spray imidacloprid, etc. to control aphids; 4) strictly observe the pesticide pre-harvest interval.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Release Trichogramma to control the Asian corn borer and apply bio-pesticides such as Bt and Beauveria bassiana; 2) intercrop or rotate with leguminous crops such as soybean and peanut to build soil fertility and reduce pests and diseases; 3) protect natural enemies in the field such as ladybirds and lacewings; 4) apply more organic fertiliser and microbial fertiliser to improve the soil microbial environment; 5) use integrated water and fertiliser management for precise supply and improved water and fertiliser use efficiency.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Practise rational crop rotation with non-gramineous crops; 2) after the autumn harvest, deep-plough to destroy stubble and bury diseased residues and overwintering pupae; 3) use black-light lamps and sex pheromone lures to trap and kill adult Asian corn borers; 4) carry out manual detasselling (detasselling alternate rows) to assist pollination and reduce nutrient consumption; 5) use plastic mulching or straw mulching to conserve moisture and regulate temperature.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) After emergence, check the stand and replant in time to ensure full and uniform emergence; 2) at the jointing stage, survey population growth and leaf area dynamics; 3) at the large trumpet stage, monitor the Asian corn borer egg masses and the rate of plants with leaf feeding damage to guide granule application; 4) at tasselling and silking, observe pollination and silk condition; 5) during grain filling, systematically monitor the dynamics of northern and southern leaf blights, grey leaf spot, rust and aphids, and apply pesticides promptly when the control threshold is reached; 6) before harvest, measure and estimate yield and keep production records.",
    },
    "健康樱桃": {
        "morphology_en": "Leaves are obovate-elliptical to ovate, 10–15 cm long, with abruptly pointed tips and sharply single-serrate margins bearing glands at the tooth tips; they are deep green and smooth (glabrous) above, with a petiole about 3 cm long bearing 2–3 purple-red nectaries, and are free of yellowing, powdery mildew and shot hole. Shoots are thick and erect, with glossy brownish-grey bark and prominent lenticels, and current-year shoots have internodes 2–4 cm long. Flower buds are large, plump and ovate with tightly clasping scales, and pollen production is abundant (most sweet cherry cultivars are self-unfruitful and require pollinator trees). Fruit are spherical or heart-shaped, with a single-fruit weight of 8–12 g (12–15 g for large-fruited cultivars), brightly coloured skin (purple-red, red-yellow, yellow), glossy with an intact bloom, firm and crisp flesh, soluble solids 16–20%, and a green stalk 3–5 cm long. The root system is well developed with many fibrous roots, mainly distributed in the 20–40 cm soil layer, free of crown gall and root rot. Trees are vigorous with a semi-spreading habit, free of gummosis and fruit-surface lesions.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "1) Temperature: according to NY/T 4788-2025 Technical Regulations for Open-field Production of Sweet Cherry, the suitable mean annual temperature is 9–14 °C and the extreme winter minimum should not fall below -18 °C; 150–200 d with a daily mean temperature above 10 °C; the daily mean temperature is 10 °C at flower-bud break, 15 °C at flowering and about 20 °C during fruit development; below -2 °C at flowering, late-frost damage occurs. The chill requirement is high: 1000–1600 chill hours (below 7.2 °C), usually 1100–1600 h for European sweet cherry; insufficient chilling causes flower-bud abortion and failure to set fruit. 2) Light: extremely light-demanding, requiring annual sunshine hours of more than 2600–2800 h; adequate light gives good fruit colouring, high sugar content and vigorous trees, while insufficient light causes excessive shoot growth and the fruiting zone to move outwards. 3) Soil: deep (≥60 cm), loose and well-aerated sandy loam or loam, pH 6.0–7.5 (optimum 6.5–7.0), total salt content <0.1%, and a groundwater table below 1.0 m in the rainy season; heavy clay, saline-alkaline and low-lying waterlogged soils must be avoided. 4) Water: neither drought-tolerant nor waterlogging-tolerant; soil water-holding capacity of 60–80% is required, with a guaranteed water supply during fruit development (from petal fall to harvest) and moderate irrigation at maturity to prevent fruit cracking, but prompt drainage after rain is essential. 5) Nutrition: apply 2000–3000 kg of decomposed organic fertiliser per mu in autumn, and top-dress before budbreak, during fruit swelling and after harvest, taking care to supplement calcium to prevent fruit cracking.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Select excellent cultivars (Hongdeng, Meizao, Brooks, Lapins, etc.) and rationally provide pollinator cultivars (Napoleon, Stella, Lapins, etc.), with main cultivar:pollinator = 2:1 or 3:1, preferably interplanting three or more cultivars; 2) use dwarfing rootstocks (the Gisela series, ZY-1) for high-density planting; 3) train to a spindle or slender spindle shape, pruning young trees lightly and leaving long branches; 4) thin flowers and fruit, retaining 3–4 fruit per flower-spur; 5) use ridge cultivation (ridge height 30 cm) with plastic film or straw mulching; 6) rain-shelter cultivation can significantly reduce fruit cracking and disease.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) Spray lime sulphur at 3–5 degrees Baumé during dormancy for orchard sanitation; 2) from budbreak to before flowering, spray difenoconazole + zhongshengmycin to prevent blossom blight and shot hole; 3) during the growing period, spray kresoxim-methyl/tebuconazole according to forecasts to control powdery mildew and thiophanate-methyl to control brown rot; 4) use insecticides to control aphids (imidacloprid), spider mites (abamectin), oriental fruit moth (chlorantraniliprole) and cherry fruit sawfly; 5) apply chemicals strictly during fruit development, stopping spraying 2–3 weeks before harvest and observing the pre-harvest interval.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Sow cover crops in the orchard (white clover, flatstem milkvetch) and protect natural enemies (predatory mites, lacewings, parasitic wasps); 2) release predatory mites (Neoseiulus californicus) to control spider mites; 3) replace chemical pesticides with bio-pesticides (Bt, matrine, Bacillus subtilis); 4) plant mint and marigold between rows to repel pests; 5) apply more microbial fertiliser to improve the rhizosphere environment.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Carry out winter orchard sanitation, removing fallen leaves and fruit and scraping off old bark; 2) whitewash the trunks to prevent freezing injury and sunscald; 3) bag the fruit or use rain-shelter cultivation to prevent fruit cracking and bird damage; 4) use bird netting (fully covered during fruit ripening); 5) release bees at flowering (mason bees or honeybees) to improve fruit set; 6) plant windbreaks in orchards exposed to wind.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) At budbreak, observe flower-bud swelling and the risk of late frost; 2) at flowering, check the arrangement of pollinator trees and insect activity; 3) during fruit development, survey powdery mildew, brown rot and fruit flies weekly; 4) before harvest, measure sugar content, colouring rate and single-fruit weight; 5) in autumn and winter, assess tree vigour by shoot length (current-year shoots of 30–50 cm indicate moderate vigour) and the defoliation date.",
    },
    "健康蓝莓": {
        "morphology_en": "A shrub with an open bush habit; canes are robust with well-filled new growth, and current-year canes are greyish-brown; leaves are simple and alternate, elliptical to oblong, deep green and glossy, turning red in autumn in some cultivars; flower buds are large and ovate, formed at the tips of fruiting canes; inflorescences are racemes with urn-shaped creamy-white flowers; fruit are spherical, blue to blue-purple, covered with a thick layer of white bloom, and uniform in size; the root system is fine with well-developed fibrous roots, lacking root hairs, with root-fungus symbiotic characteristics, and free of root rot and mummified fruit.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "A typically acid-loving plant of the Ericaceae; the most critical condition is soil acidity: highbush and lowbush blueberries are suited to soil pH 4.0–5.2, with 4.5–4.8 being optimal; rabbiteye blueberry tolerates about pH 5.5. The soil should be loose, well aerated and rich in organic matter, with an organic matter content of ≥5% and ideally 8%–12%; sandy loam or peat soil is best. It prefers moist conditions but is sensitive to both waterlogging and drought and requires convenient drainage and irrigation; air relative humidity 60%–70%. Optimum growing temperature 15–28 °C, with growth inhibited above 30 °C in summer; chill requirement: 800–1200 chill hours (below 7.2 °C) for highbush blueberry, 1000–1200 h for half-highbush and 300–600 h for rabbiteye. Abundant light favours flower-bud differentiation and fruit colouring.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Select locally adapted cultivars (northern highbush/half-highbush, southern rabbiteye/southern highbush); 2) strictly improve the soil before planting: apply 20–60 kg of sulphur powder per mu to acidify the soil, and add acidic organic matter such as peat, leaf mould and pine sawdust; 3) use ridge cultivation (ridge height 20–30 cm) with plastic film or pine-needle mulching; 4) apply acidic fertilisers such as ammonium sulphate, potassium sulphate and calcium nitrate, avoiding chlorine-containing and alkaline fertilisers such as ammonium chloride and potassium chloride; 5) supply water by drip irrigation to keep the soil moist but not waterlogged; 6) prune at the right time to control plant height and crop load; 7) sow cover crops between rows or mulch with sawdust and pine needles to maintain root-zone acidity and moisture.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) Spray lime sulphur at 3–5 degrees Baumé during dormancy for orchard sanitation; 2) during the growing period, spray preventively with mancozeb, difenoconazole, etc. to control cane blight, anthracnose and grey mould; 3) spray imidacloprid or thiamethoxam to control aphids and insecticides to control fruit flies and cranberry weevil; 4) strictly observe the pre-harvest interval during fruit ripening, giving priority to biological control; 5) avoid alkaline pesticides and foliar fertilisers containing sodium or calcium, so as not to raise the root-zone pH.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Protect and use natural enemies (parasitic wasps, predatory mites) to control insect pests; 2) apply biological control agents such as Bacillus subtilis and Trichoderma; 3) mulch the root zone with acidic organic materials such as pine needles, sawdust and rice husk to maintain soil acidity and the microbial community; 4) apply more microbial fertiliser (mycorrhizal fungi) to promote phosphorus uptake; 5) sow cover crops between rows to protect biodiversity.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Keep the root zone mulched with organic material year-round (pine needles, sawdust, pine bark); 2) use bird netting or bird-scaring tape to keep birds from pecking the fruit; 3) carry out manual pruning for ventilation and light penetration; 4) use rain-shelter cultivation to prevent fruit cracking and disease; 5) test soil pH every 1–2 years and top-dress sulphur powder to acidify the soil when it tends to alkalinity.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) In spring at budbreak, observe new shoot growth and whether the leaves are yellowing (to judge whether the pH is too high or iron is deficient); 2) at flowering, monitor flower numbers and pollination; 3) during fruit development, regularly check for fruit flies, bird damage and fruit cracking; 4) in autumn and winter, measure soil pH and organic matter and apply additional sulphur powder when necessary; 5) monitor the occurrence of cane blight and root rot.",
    },
    "健康苹果": {
        "morphology_en": "A deciduous small tree, 2–5 m tall when grafted (depending on rootstock and tree form), with an open canopy. Leaves are elliptical to ovate, 4–13 cm long and 3–7 cm wide, with acute tips and rounded bases, sharply serrate margins and pubescence along the veins beneath; they are deep green and glossy, intact and free of lesions. Current-year shoots are purple-brown and thick with moderate internodes; buds are ovate, purple-brown and densely pubescent. Fruiting occurs mainly on medium and short spurs, flower buds are plump, inflorescences are corymbs with pinkish-white petals, and pollination is adequate. Fruit are well shaped with smooth skin, even colouring and an intact bloom, firm flesh, and are free of scab lesions, ring-rot decay and orange-yellow rust spots. The root system consists of horizontal scaffold roots (distributed within the top 50 cm of soil) and vertically descending sinker roots, with milky-white fibrous roots free of decay.",
        "growing_conditions_en": "Apple prefers a cool, dry temperate climate with abundant light and is suited to cultivation between 35° and 50° N (or equivalent latitudes). 1) Temperature: the mean optimum temperature during the growing period (months 4–10, April to October) is 13–24 °C, and 18–24 °C by day with 15–17 °C at night in summer favours fruit colouring and sugar accumulation; the chill requirement is 1000–1600 chill hours (below 7.2 °C) to break dormancy normally; roots tolerate -18 °C and mature shoots and buds tolerate -30 to -40 °C, but a short frost of -2 °C at full bloom and in young fruit causes damage. 2) Light: abundant sunshine is required during the growing period (solar radiation of 2.5–4.0 GJ/m² in May to September, months 5–9), with a light penetration rate inside the canopy of ≥30%, and direct light promotes red fruit colour development. 3) Water: 500–600 mm of rainfall or irrigation, evenly distributed, is needed during the growing period; it is relatively drought-tolerant but tolerates neither prolonged drought nor waterlogging. 4) Soil: deep (≥45–60 cm), loose and fertile sandy loam or loam with good drainage is best, at soil pH 6.0–7.0 (optimum 6.5) and an organic matter content of ≥1.5%; a slope of 4%–8% with a south-facing aspect avoids frost pockets and water accumulation. 5) Mineral nutrition: N:P:K about 1:0.5:1, with attention to supplementing calcium (to prevent bitter pit), boron, zinc and other secondary and trace elements.",
        "care_agricultural_en": "1) Select excellent cultivars adapted to the area (Fuji, Gala, Delicious strains, Ruixue, etc.) and provide suitable pollinator trees (ratio 1:4 to 1:6), using vigorous virus-free nursery stock and suitable rootstocks (M9, M26, M7, Siberian crabapple, etc., chosen according to site); 2) deep-plough and improve the soil before planting and apply sufficient decomposed organic fertiliser (50–100 kg per tree); 3) carry out rational training and pruning (spindle, slender spindle or open-centre), thin flowers and fruit and control the crop load (20–25 cm between fruit); 4) apply base fertiliser in autumn and winter (organic fertiliser plus compound fertiliser) and top-dress in split applications at budbreak, the young fruit stage and fruit swelling, using drip or micro-sprinkler irrigation; 5) drain promptly after rain and irrigate in good time during drought; 6) sow cover crops between rows (white clover, ryegrass) and mow 3–4 times a year, mulching the mowings on the tree rows.",
        "care_chemical_en": "1) During dormancy (before budbreak), spray lime sulphur at 3–5 degrees Baumé or 45% amobam aqueous solution for orchard sanitation to eliminate overwintering sources of Valsa canker, ring rot and mites; 2) during the growing period, rotate protective fungicides (70% mancozeb 600–800-fold) and systemic curative fungicides (10% difenoconazole 1500–2000-fold, 43% tebuconazole 3000-fold) according to disease occurrence patterns, focusing on scab, rust, Alternaria blotch and ring rot; 3) choose insecticides according to pest occurrence: imidacloprid, thiamethoxam (aphids, fruit borers), abamectin and spirodiclofen (spider mites), taking care to protect natural enemies; 4) strictly implement the pesticide pre-harvest interval and the maximum number of applications, and select low-toxicity, residue-free products for sprays before fruit bagging.",
        "care_ecological_en": "1) Practise cover cropping in the orchard and retain natural vegetation strips to increase biodiversity; 2) protect and release natural enemies such as ladybirds, lacewings, hoverflies, parasitic wasps and predatory mites, and minimise the use of broad-spectrum insecticides; 3) apply bio-pesticides such as Bacillus subtilis, Trichoderma, Bt and Beauveria bassiana; 4) apply more bio-organic fertiliser and EM microbial fertiliser to improve the rhizosphere micro-ecology; 5) establish windbreaks around the orchard to reduce wind speed and sunscald.",
        "care_physical_en": "1) Carry out winter orchard sanitation, scraping off old and loose bark and Valsa canker lesions, and whitewash the trunks (quicklime:sulphur:salt:water = 10:1:1:30) to prevent freezing injury and pests; 2) bag the fruit (bagging 30–40 days after flowering and removing the bags 15–20 days before harvest for colouring); 3) band the trunks with straw to attract overwintering pests, then remove and burn the bands in early spring; 4) use sugar-vinegar liquid, sex pheromone lures (peach fruit moth, oriental fruit moth) and yellow sticky traps for trapping and killing; 5) frost protection: in the event of low temperature at flowering, use smoke, water spraying or wind machines to drive off frost.",
        "care_monitoring_en": "1) At flowering, observe budbreak, flowering and pollination and record late-frost damage; 2) from the young fruit stage to the swelling stage, survey fixed trees for the dynamics of scab, rust, aphids, spider mites and fruit borers; 3) during fruit colouring, monitor spider mites, Alternaria blotch and ring rot; 4) in autumn and winter, survey tree vigour, Valsa canker lesions and the timing of defoliation to guide pruning, fertilisation and orchard sanitation; 5) keep orchard production records of weather, cultivation operations and control decisions.",
    },
}
